      "math_academy_id": "q-343249",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "Let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=-\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}+1$ defined on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of the Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $G(x)=-9x$ from the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}+1∼1-\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{18(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}}{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}}\nand\n{{field-3}}\non $R,$ we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n-9x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-4}}\\cdot {{field-5}}.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=\\pi $ and we are already given the coefficients ${a}_{0}$ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼1-\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{18(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-\\pi ,\\pi ),$\n\n- continuous on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-\\pi )=F(\\pi ),$ if we extend the value at $x=\\pi $ using the formula $F(x)=-\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}+1.$\n\nTherefore, the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{continuous}$ and $\\text{piecewise smooth}$ on $R,$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}+1) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1-\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{2})+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{18(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)) \\\\ -9x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{18(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot (-n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)) \\\\ -9x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-18(-1)^{n+1}}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx) \\\\ -9x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{n}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
